          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            The concept of
            %em adjoint functors
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Kan58"}> Kan (1958)
            \&amp;rbrack; is a key concept in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            — if not
            %em the
            key concept — and it is in large part through the manifold identification of examples of adjoint functors appearing ubiquitously in the practice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            that category theoretic tools are brought to use in general mathematics.
          %p
            Abstractly, the notion of adjoint functors embodies the concept of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functors"} representable functors
            and has as special cases the fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            such as notably
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extensions"} Kan extensions
            and hence
            %em
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"}> co-
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"}> co-
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ends"} ends
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            which in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+theory"} 2-category theory
            embodies a general
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics+--+categories+and+toposes#CategoryTheoryIsTheoryOfDuality"} principle of duality
          %p
            Concretely, the concept of adjoint functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi R
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ⇆
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \dashv R \,\colon\, \mathcal{D} \leftrightarrows \mathcal{C}
            is immediately transparent and compelling in its incarnation as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-sets"} hom-sets
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#InTermsOfHomIsomorphism"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-objects"} hom-objects
            (see at
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+adjoint+functor"} enriched adjoint functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+condition"} coherently
            be switched left
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ↔
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leftrightarrow
            right in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi R
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
