              %p
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p The central point about examples of adjoint functors is:
          %p
            %em Adjoint functors are ubiquitous
            \.
          %p
            To a fair extent,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is all about adjoint functors and the other
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+construction"} universal construction
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
          %p
            Listing examples of adjoint functors is much like listing examples of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            for more).
          %p Keeping that in mind, we do list some special cases and special classes of examples that are useful to know. But any list is necessarily wildly incomplete.
          %h3#general General
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A pair of adjoint functors between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+correspondence"} Galois correspondence
            %li
              %p
                A pair of adjoint functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi L
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L \dashv R)
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful functor
                exhibits a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
              %p
                In this case
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                may be regarded as a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi c
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : c \to R d
                into a local object factors through the localization of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            %li
              %p
                A pair of adjoint functors that is also an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
                is called an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+equivalence"} adjoint equivalence
            %li
              %p
                A pair of adjoint functors where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                have finite
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                preserves these finite limits is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
                = succeed "es." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                is full and faithful, then this is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+embedding"} geometric embedding
            %li
              %p
                The left and right adjoint functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_*
                (if they exist) to a functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi p
